@page "/about"

@* The entry to the explainers. Each file format gets a card here and its own page below
   about/. Same writing style as those pages: simple English, short sentences, no hyphens,
   underscores or dashes in the visible text. *@

<PageTitle>About · Vault of FAF</PageTitle>

<section class="py-10 sm:py-14">
    <p class="font-display text-sm font-bold tracking-[0.2em] text-primary">ABOUT</p>
    <h1 class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-4xl font-extrabold tracking-tight sm:text-5xl">How Vault of FAF reads your games</h1>
    <p class="mt-5 max-w-2xl text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
        Vault of FAF opens the files of Supreme Commander: Forged Alliance right in your browser.
        These pages explain how that works. They are written for players, not for programmers.
    </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="formats">
    <h2 id="formats" class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-ink-faint">File formats</h2>
    <div class="mt-4 grid gap-6 md:grid-cols-2">
        <a href="about/replay-format" class="card group relative flex flex-col overflow-hidden transition hover:border-primary hover:shadow-[0_0_60px_-24px_var(--th-glow)]">
            @* The first bytes of a replay spell the name of the game. *@
            <div class="flex gap-1.5 font-mono text-sm" aria-hidden="true">
                @foreach (byte value in ExampleReplay.FirstBytes.Take(7))
                {
                    <span class="flex w-9 flex-col items-center rounded-md border border-edge bg-raised py-1.5">
                        <span class="text-ink-muted">@value.ToString("X2")</span>
                        <span class="font-semibold text-primary transition group-hover:scale-125">@((char)value)</span>
                    </span>
                }
                <span class="self-center pl-1 text-ink-faint">…</span>
            </div>
            <h3 class="mt-6 text-2xl font-bold text-ink">The replay format</h3>
            <p class="mt-1 font-mono text-sm text-primary">.fafreplay · .scfareplay</p>
            <p class="mt-3 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
                What is inside a replay, how we read it byte by byte, and how a long row of numbers
                turns into build orders, APM and chat.
            </p>
            <p class="mt-6 text-sm font-semibold text-primary">
                Read the story <span class="inline-block transition group-hover:translate-x-1" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
            </p>
        </a>

        <div class="card flex flex-col justify-center border-dashed bg-transparent text-center">
            <p class="font-display text-lg font-bold text-ink-muted">More to come</p>
            <p class="mx-auto mt-2 max-w-sm text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-faint">
                The game has more files worth a look. They will get their own page here.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-2xl text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
    <h2 class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-ink-faint">This site</h2>
    <p class="mt-3">
        Vault of FAF is open source. All of the reading and counting happens on your own computer, in your browser.
        The code is on <a href="@BuildInfo.Repository" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" class="text-primary hover:underline">GitHub</a>.
    </p>
</section>
